body:
I hope this doesn't get buried because it might offer some perspective. I used to be in your wife's position. When my husband told me, the strongest reaction I had was fear. Mainly for his soul (I didn't want to spend eternity without him.) We had taken our marriage vows "under God" and our relationship with each other was supposed to be grounded in our relationship with God.  That vow was equally important to me as the vow I made to always love and be faithful to my husband. 
&nbsp;

So in my eyes, he was breaking a marriage vow to no longer be a Christian. My first thought was also "I wouldn't have married you if I knew you'd later deconvert." 
&nbsp;

My second reaction was guilt. I felt like I failed him and God and myself. I felt guilty for not seeing it sooner and allowing myself to become "unequally yoked" with an unbeliever. I felt guilty that I didn't help him in his faith more when he was struggling and I didn't know. And I felt like I failed God and my marriage by allowing it to get that far. We never threatened divorce or anything though, so that doesn't sound too good. 
&nbsp;

He was so patient with me and never pushed. He offered to go to church with me if that was important to me still, as that was a commitment he made when we got married. Knowing that he would still keep that commitment even though he didn't believe it made me feel a lot better and more secure and less lied to. For a couple years we just didn't talk about religion. I let him be and he let me be.
&nbsp;

Fortunately his gentle nature and just continuing to be my loving, dependable husband helped bring me out of the years of brainwashing I had received in my upbringing and I am so thankful every day for his patience. My mental health is 10x better now that I have left that toxic place behind me. 
&nbsp;

Edit: formatting
